<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hiero II of Syracuse</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 308–215 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Hieron II of Syracuse</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hiero II of Syracuse was a general who seized power in Syracuse and took the title of king after defeating the Mamertines at the Longanus river around 265 BCE. Early in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-punic-war">First Punic War</a> he allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 263 BCE and remained a loyal ally until his death. His long reign brought Syracuse prosperity, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archimedes">Archimedes</a> worked at his court.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hiero-ii-of-syracuse.jpg" alt="Hiero II of Syracuse" width="320" height="317" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stella · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moneta_Gerone_II.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
